+---
+id: dl-004-applymergepatch-semantics-strict-rfc-7386-non-mutating
+type: decision-log
+title: "applyMergePatch semantics: strict RFC 7386, non-mutating"
+status: pending
+---
+
+<!-- decision-log body. `wingfoil memory add` copies this scaffold verbatim; `memory submit` replaces
+     these placeholder comments with real content and fills the required frontmatter fields. -->
+
+## Context
+
+Clients send partial updates as JSON Merge Patch (RFC 7386). The library already has `applyPatch`
+(dl-002) with a non-mutating, strict contract.
+
+## Decision
+
+- `applyMergePatch(target, patch)` follows the RFC's MergePatch pseudo-code exactly: a non-object patch
+  (array, null, scalar) replaces the target; an object patch merges member by member, null removes,
+  arrays are replaced and never merged, and a non-object target is treated as `{}` (so nulls nested in
+  a patch applied to a missing member are dropped, as the RFC's test cases require).
+- Same contract as `applyPatch`: neither argument is mutated and the result shares no structure with
+  them (`structuredClone`). Members are written with `defineProperty`, so `__proto__` stays an ordinary
+  member. Member order is stable: existing keys keep their position, new ones follow in patch order.
+- Never throws: every JSON value is a valid patch, so there is no malformed input to reject.
+
+## Consequences
+
+A null in a merge patch cannot set a member to null (inherent to RFC 7386). Callers needing that must
+use `applyPatch`.
